// This file contains functions which are useful to use with physicals.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  limexp — exponential growth limiter, written entirely in Hover.
//
//  Caps exp(x)'s growth past a threshold by switching to a linear
//  extrapolation that matches both the VALUE and SLOPE of exp(x) at the
//  threshold -- d/dx exp(x) = exp(x), so the same constant (e^threshold)
//  serves as both the seam value and the linear segment's slope. This
//  keeps the function and its derivative CONTINUOUS at the seam: no kink,
//  no new non-smoothness for Newton to trip over, which would defeat the
//  purpose of a convergence aid.
//
//  Use this in place of a bare exp() call anywhere a Newton iteration
//  might propose a wild intermediate value before settling -- exactly the
//  situation that caused runaway blowup in BJT/diode-style models with
//  exp(v/Vt) terms, confirmed directly in this project's own testing.
//
//  No compiler/elaborator changes needed: this is ordinary Hover source,
//  using the same func mechanism already used for this project's own
//  exp() helper. Just import this file and call limexp(x) instead of
//  exp(x) wherever a model's exponential term is the suspected source of
//  convergence trouble.
//
//  threshold=80.0 is chosen generously large -- exp(80) ~ 5.5e34, already
//  far beyond any physically meaningful voltage/current ratio for a real
//  circuit, so well-behaved iterations near the true solution are
//  completely unaffected. Only a Newton guess that has gone genuinely
//  far off the rails gets capped.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

func double limexp(double x) {
    double threshold = 80.0;

    if (x < threshold) {
        return exp(x);
    }

    double e_t = exp(threshold);
    return e_t * (1.0 + (x - threshold));
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pnjlim() — Newton-Raphson junction voltage limiter (Berkeley formulation)
//
// Bounds how far a junction voltage may move in one Newton iteration, so an
// iterate that wants to leap several decades of exponential current advances
// by a bounded amount instead. It limits the STEP, not the device: the
// converged solution still lies on the true exponential I-V, because at
// convergence the step is small and the limiter is in pass-through.
//
// Parameters:
//   v_new  : proposed junction voltage for this iteration
//   v_old  : the previous iteration's voltage. Obtain it with nr_prev(), e.g.
//              pnjlim(V(a) - V(b), nr_prev(V(a) - V(b)), nvt, vcrit)
//            nr_prev() and NOT a plain read: this needs the previous ITERATE,
//            which is exactly what nr_prev() reports.
//   nvt    : thermal voltage times the junction's emission coefficient
//   v_crit : voltage at which the exponential's curvature starts to dominate,
//            nvt*log(nvt/(sqrt(2)*Is)). Below it no limiting is applied.
//
// Two guards mean it does nothing in the common case: anything below v_crit
// passes through, and so does any step smaller than 2*nvt. Past both, the step
// is compressed logarithmically.
//
// The v_old > 0 branches are mirror images of each other. That symmetry is the
// property the previous implementation of this function lacked: it computed
// arg = 1 + dv/vt and fell back to v_crit whenever arg went non-positive —
// which happens precisely on a large DOWNWARD step, so a junction retreating
// from a bad guess was answered with a jump UP to v_crit, large and in the
// wrong direction. It also never engaged at all while v_new <= v_crit, however
// violent the step.
//
// A NOTE ON THE SEAM AT |dv| = 2*nvt, AND A FIX THAT DID NOT WORK
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This branch is discontinuous where it meets the pass-through guard above.
// Any |dv| < 2*nvt passes straight through, so the limited branch is entered
// at arg = 2+, where log(arg - 2) -> -infinity: at arg = 2.0001 pass-through
// returns 2*nvt above v_old while this branch returns 7.2*nvt BELOW it, with a
// local slope of 10000 rather than 1.
//
// That matters more in Hover than in SPICE. SPICE excludes its limiter from
// the Jacobian — $limit moves the operating point and dI/dV is taken with
// respect to the unlimited voltage — whereas this function sits in the analog
// block's ordinary data flow, so its slope is part of the derivative the
// solver gets. (That is true of automatic differentiation too; it is not an
// artefact of finite differences.)
//
// Shifting the log by one — log(arg - 1) — removes the discontinuity in both
// value and slope: at arg = 2 it returns exactly v_old + 2*nvt with slope 1,
// matching pass-through on both counts, and it agrees with Berkeley to a
// fraction of nvt far from the seam (6.6347 vs 6.6444 at arg = 105).
//
// It was tried, and it is NOT an improvement in practice. It did not fix the
// full-bridge rectifier it was aimed at, and it broke examples/BJT/npn_amp.hvr,
// which stopped converging at t = 0 having previously run in 3165 steps. The
// big downward jump this branch makes near the seam is evidently doing useful
// work on hard cold starts, however ugly it looks. Berkeley's form is restored
// here; do not "fix" the discontinuity again without re-running npn_amp.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
func double pnjlim(double v_new, double v_old, double nvt, double v_crit) {
    if (v_new <= v_crit) { return v_new; }

    double dv = v_new - v_old;
    if (dv < 2.0 * nvt && dv > -2.0 * nvt) { return v_new; }

    if (v_old > 0.0) {
        double arg = dv / nvt;
        if (arg > 2.0) {
            return v_old + nvt * (2.0 + log(arg - 2.0));
        }
        // arg < -2 here, so -2 - arg > 0 and the log is always defined.
        return v_old - nvt * (2.0 + log(-2.0 - arg));
    }

    // No usable forward history (previous iterate at or below zero). Re-enter
    // the exponential region from its own scale rather than from wherever the
    // solver's guess landed. v_new > v_crit > 0 from the first branch, so the
    // log argument is positive.
    return nvt * log(v_new / nvt);
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  pn-junction model math
//
//  Everything below is shared by every junction device in the standard library
//  — the diode, both BJT polarities, and the MOSFET body diodes. It lives here
//  rather than in <semiconductors> because none of it knows what device it is
//  in: these are the functions a junction has, not the functions a diode or a
//  transistor has, and pnjlim() above is already the same kind of thing.
//
//  There is a second reason, specific to how Hover names things. Functions are
//  namespaced per DIRECTORY (compiler/elaborator/scope.go), so two device
//  families that each kept a private copy would compile — the elaborator gives
//  them distinct C identifiers — and would then silently drift apart. One copy
//  here is one definition of what a depletion charge is.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// jexp — junction exponential, exp(v/nvt) with an overflow guard.
//
// The guard is on the ARGUMENT rather than on the voltage, so the threshold
// scales with each junction's own emission coefficient rather than assuming
// room-temperature silicon. e^60 ~ 1.1e26 is far outside any physical bias
// yet still e^222 short of double overflow.
//
// The ceiling is 60, not the historical 40, and the reason is knee voltage,
// not overflow: the guard must sit ABOVE the operating knee of every device
// in the library, because a hard clamp's zero-slope region is a zero column
// in the measured Jacobian (vm_compute_jacobian perturbs node voltages and
// re-runs this exact source), and a zero column is a direction Newton cannot
// step along. At 40, the guard engaged at 40*nvt = 1.98 V for the red LED
// (nvt ~ 49.5 mV) — 130 mV past its 1.85 V operating knee — so the Jacobian
// went flat exactly where a driven source crossing the knee needs it most,
// and the OP ladder cycled on that knee forever (measured on redled.hvr:
// rungs 0.40+ exhausted 500 iterations each). At 60 the same LED clamps at
// 2.97 V and a silicon diode at 1.56 V, both clear of the knee with e^60
// still nowhere near overflow.
//
// The guard is a hard clamp, and in Hover a hard clamp is not a neutral act.
// That is why device models must not clamp their own terminal voltages.
//
// Smooth extrapolation was tried in its place, matching value and slope at the
// seam the way limexp() does, and it was worse: past the seam the current then
// grows without bound and a bad iterate runs away instead of saturating. This
// is the one place where flat beats smooth.
func double jexp(double v, double nvt) {
    double x = v / nvt;
    if (x >  60.0) { x =  60.0; }
    if (x < -40.0) { x = -40.0; }
    return exp(x);
}

// vcrit_of — the junction voltage at which the exponential's curvature starts
// to dominate, nvt*log(nvt/(sqrt(2)*Is)). This is the v_crit that pnjlim()
// wants: below it an unlimited Newton step is harmless, above it the curve is
// steep enough that one unbounded step can overshoot by decades of current.
func double vcrit_of(double Is, double nvt) {
    double arg = nvt / (1.41421356237 * Is);
    if (arg < 1.0) { return 0.0; }   // absurdly large Is: limit everywhere
    return nvt * log(arg);
}

// q_dep — depletion charge, the integral of the junction capacitance law
// Cj/(1 - v/Vj)^Mj.
//
// Below Fc*Vj that integral is closed-form. Above it the capacitance law is
// replaced by its tangent line, because the true law has a pole at v = Vj and
// a forward-biased junction sits right on top of it — pow() would be handed a
// negative base and return NaN. The F1/F2/F3 grouping is SPICE's, and it is
// built so that BOTH the charge and its slope match at the v = Fc*Vj seam: a
// kink there would be a step in capacitance, which the solver feels as a
// spurious current spike every time a junction crosses it.
//
// Feed this the UNLIMITED junction voltage. Hover measures dq/dv numerically,
// through whatever the caller did to get its argument, so passing a pnjlim()
// output makes the limiter's compression divide the capacitance — and a
// junction that presents almost no capacitance during a violent iterate is
// exactly a junction that will not converge. Limiting exists to tame the
// exponential; this power law needs none of it.
func double q_dep(double v, double Cj, double Vj, double Mj, double Fc) {
    if (Cj <= 0.0) { return 0.0; }

    double vf = Fc * Vj;
    if (v < vf) {
        return Cj * Vj / (1.0 - Mj) * (1.0 - pow(1.0 - v / Vj, 1.0 - Mj));
    }

    double f1 = Vj * (1.0 - pow(1.0 - Fc, 1.0 - Mj)) / (1.0 - Mj);
    double f2 = pow(1.0 - Fc, 1.0 + Mj);
    double f3 = 1.0 - Fc * (1.0 + Mj);
    return Cj * (f1 + (f3 * (v - vf)
                       + (Mj / (2.0 * Vj)) * (v * v - vf * vf)) / f2);
}

// rb_of — current-dependent base resistance (Gummel-Poon).
//
// A real base is not a resistor: as injection rises the current crowds toward
// the emitter edge, the conducting path shortens, and the effective resistance
// falls from RB toward the high-current floor RBM. Two formulations, matching
// SPICE's:
//
//   Irb <= 0   the base-charge form, Rbm + (Rb - Rbm)/qb — cheap, and tied to
//              the same qb that already carries Early and high injection.
//   Irb  > 0   the Berkeley current form, where Irb is the base current at
//              which Rb has fallen about half way to Rbm.
//
// The z-series has a removable singularity at ib -> 0: (tan z - z)/(z tan^2 z)
// tends to 1/3, which is why the small-z branch returns Rb exactly rather than
// evaluating 0/0. At the other end z approaches pi/2 from below and the same
// group tends to 0, so the result tends to Rbm. Both limits are correct by
// construction.
//
// This one is only used by the BJTs, but it is junction-model math of the same
// kind and splitting it off would put half of one model in another package.
func double rb_of(double Rb, double Rbm, double Irb, double ib, double qb) {
    if (Rbm <= 0.0) { return Rb; }    // RBM unset: constant base resistance
    if (Rbm >= Rb)  { return Rbm; }

    if (Irb <= 0.0) {
        if (qb < 1.0e-9) { return Rb; }
        return Rbm + (Rb - Rbm) / qb;
    }

    if (ib <= 0.0) { return Rb; }
    double x = ib / Irb;
    double z = (-1.0 + sqrt(1.0 + 14.5924348 * x)) / (2.4317084 * sqrt(x));
    if (z < 1.0e-4) { return Rb; }
    double tz = tan(z);
    return Rbm + 3.0 * (Rb - Rbm) * (tz - z) / (z * tz * tz);
}
